Subject: [PATCH] cmd: cmd /c returns the exit code of the last command, not ERRORLEVEL.

On Windows, "cmd /c script.bat" exits with the exit code of the last command the script
ran. ECHO, GOTO and other internal commands that leave ERRORLEVEL alone still end with 0,
so a script whose last line is ECHO succeeds even after a failed command (Wine's own tests
expect this: "cmd.exe /c sel.bat 458" gives 0 although ERRORLEVEL is 458, and a block
script ending in ECHO after an unknown command gives "0 ok"). Wine returned ERRORLEVEL
whenever the last command succeeded.

Red Giant / Maxon installers run preflight scripts like
    echo "Stopping MxContentService.exe..."
    taskkill /f /im "MxContentService.exe"
    echo "MxContentService.exe stopped."
taskkill returns 128 when the service is not running (as on Windows), and Wine's cmd
returned that 128, so Magic Bullet Suite and Universe failed with "Status 11015:
err.sys.script-execution-failed: Script execution failed for task: preflight".
(The binary patch in lib/patch_runner.py turns the "!= NO_ERROR" jump into a plain jump.)
---
--- a/programs/cmd/wcmdmain.c
+++ b/programs/cmd/wcmdmain.c
@@ -4968,7 +4968,9 @@
     {
         RETURN_CODE return_code = WCMD_call_batch(NULL, parameters.initial_command);
         if (return_code == RETURN_CODE_GOTO) return NO_ERROR;
-        if (return_code != RETURN_CODE_ABORTED && return_code != RETURN_CODE_EXITED && return_code != NO_ERROR)
+        /* The exit code is the last command's, not ERRORLEVEL: a script whose last command
+         * is ECHO exits with 0 even after a command that failed (native does the same). */
+        if (return_code != RETURN_CODE_ABORTED && return_code != RETURN_CODE_EXITED)
             return return_code;
         return errorlevel;
     }
